{"author":{"id":"8044233cc21d4498a49e4a5a452ed824a81830d46e1fdc556b7dd44b12149dae"},"ops":[{"type":2,"timestamp":1586595074,"nonce":"SoKeogKBQl9dBvZeaS8bkBpTx9o=","metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MzIyMzg0MDk5Nw=="},"title":"Open/close operations are too easy to do and lead to dupplicate","was":"Open/close operations are too easy to do and lead to dupplicate"},{"type":3,"timestamp":1586595215,"nonce":"9bHC1YFQPTM4R61K3+pKhZ1mIds=","metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDYxMjM3MDQzOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug/issues/367#issuecomment-612370438"},"message":"Yeah, that could be done better indeed. Not sure what's the best solution though, it might be just an UX issue. Here is what I see as possible fix:\n- Finish the transaction concept (https://github.com/MichaelMure/git-bug/issues/337) and give more control to UIs over what is pending and allow to revert\n- Have the status change be \"less easy\":\n  - have a \"confirm\" step ?\n  - as the status will eventually have more values than open/closed, have a secondary UI to select the value\n- have a way to \"undo\", but that implies knowing what *can* be undo as you must not remove operation that were already propagated. This could be limited to the pending ops. There is also a more general notion here of *editing* the pending operation (especially comments).","files":null},{"type":5,"timestamp":1586595321,"nonce":"j++klFO5LAe5Z6HWgdzXnSfEaoc=","metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDMyMjM4NDQyMDA="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"timestamp":1586595328,"nonce":"C210QY6ZhH4ZtC4yZINmtM4RUes=","metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDMyMjM4NDQzMTQ="},"added":["area/ui/terminal"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"timestamp":1586595328,"nonce":"TzJkvYws2nVCuplJaI26MzTwE0A=","metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDMyMjM4NDQzMTU="},"added":["area/ui/web"],"removed":[]}]}